In article <2e0bth$1cd@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu>, rt10405@eehpx34 (Ronald F Talaga Jr) writes:

> gambit@uxa.cso.uiuc.edu (Between the Wheels) writes:

>>What's up with this crap???  Although I'll have to admit, I love how they
>>decided to keep the .d group.

> I also like how they kept the alt.binaries.pictures.misc group.  If they are
> going to remove a type of group for any reason, they should be consistent.
> Smells like censorship to me.

I'm the news admin for the University of Pittsburgh.

I find your posting rather interesting if the people in question are
claiming that they "must" drop abpe because of copyright violations.

From my own surveys done for the University of Pittsburgh, I found that if
one is going to drop any group for copyright violations, the "misc" group
should be the first to go.  There are far more "pirate" scan there than in
abpe.

As an aside, I'll note that whenever I get a complaint about a picture group
it's abpe.  It seems that abpe is the ONLY picture group that complainers ever
know of.  You'd swear is was the only one that "defenders of copyrights" read.

People will swear up and down that they don't have a problem with "erotica".
Rather they argue that intellectual property needs to be protected.  So they
demand that abpe be removed.  When I point out that there are other groups,
such as the binary sound group, that "suffer" from the same "problem" and
should be removed as well, they usually drop their demand(s).

Also, I point out that groups such as soc.* have the occasional posting that
starts "Reprinted without permission".  I ask, "should we remove these
groups as well?"  Usually the "defenders of copyrights" argue that such
groups have a lower percentage of "stolen" material so it's OK to keep these
minor transgressor groups.  In that case, I ask what percentage constitutes
the point where a group should be removed?

This is why such decisions about what to keep and what to "ban" are left to
faculty committees here at Pitt.  I perfer to let them justify these decisons.
It also gives the university libraries a chance to give some input.

BTW if you are looking for an ally in restoring newsgroups to a server, do
see if your university library is aware that the computer center is "banning"
newsgroups.  Sometimes a university library realizes that if an information
source such as a newsgroup can be "banned" then so can books in their
library.  Many universities have policies in place that protect their
libraries from arbitrary "banning" of books.  Many times these policies can
be extended to newsgroups as well.  It's worth a look.  You might even
contact your library science department and see how they view this "banning".

I would like to see all the groups clean up their collective "acts".  Tim
Hester can attest that I've raised the issue of copyrights before in abpe.
These groups should have original material in them.  Those people who
complain that "there would be no traffic at all" if it weren't for pirated
scans are the ones who hand eroto-phobes the opportunity to ban their
favorite newsgroup.

No, dropping Playboy magazine into the scanner and posting it to the net is
no different from dropping Playboy magazine into the color copier and making
10,000 copies and giving them away to friends.  This is clearly wrong.

But "banning" abpe, while keeping the "misc" group, clearly shows that the
people in question have a problem with "erotica", not copyrights.

Terry

