From: george@lisa.cso.uiuc.edu (george badger)
Newsgroups: uiuc.general
Subject:  pictures
Date: 6 Dec 1993 22:20:32 GMT
Message-ID: <2e0b7g$9k@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu>

For a long time CCSO had the practice of not bringing in picture  
newsgroups, based originally on a capacity criteria when disk was  
expensive and scarce. For non picture groups the general practice was to  
bring in at least everything people asked for, with the retention practice  
being used to manage space. As the space crunch got relieved, Paul took  
the initiative to expand this to include requests for picture groups, but  
without asking for any management approval. I have no objection to this,  
as part of his job is to be responsive within available resources and  
policies.  When the most looked at list was published this became visible,  
and in the followup discussion it became clear that space was not a  
current critical problem, nor was network bandwidth, but that extensive  
copyright violation was. This made my decision to return to the old  
practice easy, but leaves open the issues of uncopyrighted material.

I call these practices rather than policies because there are no  policies  
to exclude any material based on content, other than that which is  
illegal. The only legal issue which has been raised has been the copyright  
one on which the University has taken the stand of complying with the law  
in all forms of distribution, most explicitly photocopying. We cannot be a  
knowing agent in assisting copyright violation. 

There are, however, policies which can come into play now or in the  
future. First, we give priority in the use of campus funded student  
computing to things related to education. (Note: This campus funding comes  
from state appropriations of general revenue and tuition income.) Second,  
we put a high priority on student access to computing at their own  
initiative and for their own purposes. Combining these, we would give  
precedence to resource demands backed by an academic argument over those  
for entertainment, but would support both when feasible and legal. Other  
operators of computers on campus might come to different decisions based  
on their sources of funds.

Another policy/practice is to try and provide services that we can support  
well with available capacity or capacity we can forecast. While we have  
moved from a disk shortage for still pictures to adequate capacity, we can  
easily foresee the availability of video in the near future, where both  
network and storage capacities will again be stressed. Academic  
relatedness will probably have to be a criteria. Note that none of this is  
related to other criteria such as offensiveness or merit, areas where we  
have not tried to establish guidelines. As more material has to be paid  
for, such as various electronic publications, something akin to a library  
collections process will have to be used.

Has there  been any pressure on this topic?  None, either formally or  
informally. Only two related issues have come up in the past several  
months. In one case a department asked for a recommendation on what to do  
about staff collecting large amounts of pictures which were presenting  
operational problems for their staff and interfering with the purpose for  
which their equipment was purchased. In the other, which was well  
publicized in this forum, a campus group raised concerns about the impact  
of displaying pictures that some found offensive in a manner that imposed  
them on other users (a hostile workplace analog). I see little sympathy  
for any form of institutional censorship on this campus, either from  
campus officials or from the community at large.

As for who is accountable for such decisions, it is normally department  
heads and directors who are directly accountable and expected to make  
decisions within whatever policies and traditions exist. They are always  
subject to higher level campus officials and ultimately the Trustees, as  
well as to the public debate that can ensue both on and off campus. 

For the sake of completeness I would point out that if content were the  
criterion any number of political, religious, sexual, hobby or other news  
groups are equally offensive to some, and at least frivolous to others.  
There has never been any interest in limiting access to these.
