rolf@geoserv.isgs.uiuc.edu (Rolf Wilson) writes:

>   Other people have said it, but I will repeat it. It is not "censorship"
>for the University to choose or not choose to carry a given service, any
>more than it is censorship for a bookstore to decide not to sell a book.
>Please don't use that word again - it is not appropriate.
[...]

The word "censorship" is in the Student Code. It is also a common term
in library policy (which to me seems a better model for netnews policy
that bookstore policy).

Having said that, I would not call it university censorship in this
case if the articles were removed for good legal reasons. A discussion
before the removal with information from a competent legal advisor
would have been a good way to establish publicly if there were good
legal reasons for removal.

Even if you trust, Paul Pomes and George Badger (and I think most of
us do), can we be sure that future folks with their jobs can be
trusted with the authority to decide what and what is legal material
without any outside discussion and expert advice? I don't think so.


- Carl

