Subject: An interesting problem ..
Date: 9 Dec 1993 10:10:37 -0500
Message-ID: <28B674F368A@night.vtc.vsc.edu>


Ok... we've got an interesting problem.

I'm sysop on a BBS at Vermont Technical College (VTC). We at VTC have a
connection to the Internet and we want to make the Internet accessible to
our calling audience. We have set up a Telnet door, an FTP door, and a
gopher door. Thus our callers can get remote terminals on various systems
all over the Internet, download files from many FTP archives and browse
various gophers. We also want to support the ability to email to and from
Internet addresses and *maybe* read the USENET newsgroups.

Needless to say these facilities are quite popular with our callers. Yeah!

However.

The VTC administration is now talking about forcing us to discontinue these
services because Internet access brings with it the possibility of a minor
accessing "unsuitable" (read "pornographic") material. For example a 12
year old caller might Telnet to a MUD where he could create a character and
wander around in a role-playing universe where, among other things, people
occasionally have virtual sex.

Or a caller might download multiple megabytes of pornographic .GIF files
from an FTP site in... who knows... Sweden. Or a caller might subscribe to
the rec.arts.erotica newsgroup... or perhaps the alt.sex.fetish.watersports
group.

You see the problem.

From a legal point of view, I'm not sure where VTC would stand on this.
After all... the files were not provided by VTC... they were download from
a remote site that VTC has no control over. Furthermore, since some of
these services are probably available in other ways (FidoNet, for
example?), it doesn't make sense to blame the local board for stuff
provided by remote systems that the local board can access.

On the other hand because the Internet is *normally* only available to
commercial and government organizations and college age students, FTP
archives don't worry about access by minors; it would be unreasonable to
expect them to deal with the issue.

VTC is also concerned about public opinion should a parent throw a fit when
they find out their son/daughter is downloading pornographic .GIF files
"from" The VTC BBS.

Comments? Thoughts? Advice?

Thanks in advance!

Peter Chapin and Michael Martel
