In article <28B674F368A@night.vtc.vsc.edu>, MMARTEL@night.vtc.vsc.edu
(Michael Martel aka Magnus) wrote:

> The VTC administration is now talking about forcing us to discontinue these
> services because Internet access brings with it the possibility of a minor
> accessing "unsuitable" (read "pornographic") material. For example a 12
> year old caller might Telnet to a MUD where he could create a character and
> wander around in a role-playing universe where, among other things, people
> occasionally have virtual sex.
> 
> Or a caller might download multiple megabytes of pornographic .GIF files
> from an FTP site in... who knows... Sweden. Or a caller might subscribe to
> the rec.arts.erotica newsgroup... or perhaps the alt.sex.fetish.watersports
> group.

 . . . .

> On the other hand because the Internet is *normally* only available to
> commercial and government organizations and college age students, FTP
> archives don't worry about access by minors; it would be unreasonable to
> expect them to deal with the issue.

This is going to be a continuing problem for a while, as more kids get
Internet access!  In fact, I'd argue that your last point is not really
true.  Lots of people are sensitive to the increasing number of minors with
Internet access, both as individuals and via public libraries and schools.

One thing you might consider is requiring a permission form from parents. 
(That's assuming you can reliably identify who's a minor and who's not--not
trivial on a BBS!)  Let them know up front that, while you have made an
effort to keep sexually explicit materials off the BBS, that it is possible
to find them elsewhere, and a clever person who wants to find them will,
and have them sign saying that they will not hold VTC responsible in case
of the kid finding something offensive.  Make it clear that you're not
responsible for what the kids find elsewhere, anymore than the local
transit authority is responsible if somebody rides one of their buses to
get to a bank which they intend to rob.

Another thing would be to sit down with some people from your
administration and demonstrate to them how one might acquire some naughty
stuff over the net.  In other words, track down a news server that carries,
say, alt.binaries.pictures.erotica and will let you connect to it, get a
uuencoded GIF, find the software to decode it, extract and decode the file,
find a GIF viewer, and check it out.  In other words, drive home the point
that this is not as simple as picking up a copy of Playboy off a newsstand,
but that you have to make a concerted effort to find and view the stuff. 
At the same time, demonstrate some of the things on the net that the kids
could benefit from.  At the least, they will then hopefully make a decision
based on some real experience and not on sensationalized newpaper accounts.

The real problem is that no matter what you do, somebody sometime will find
something they don't like (or their kids will).  It's easy to toss around
words like "offensive" and forget that anything will probably offend
somebody.  Forget sex--what about religion and politics?  There's a lot of
stuff out there which, while not obscene by any standard, some parents
would probably not want their kids to read.  For instance, on
comp.sys.mac.advocacy there has been a discussion about Apple's policy on
benefits for domestic partners and the heat they're taking for it.  
-- 
