In article <2e3ivv$f1k@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu>, hucke@sumter.cso.uiuc.edu (Matt Hucke) writes:

> cburian@ux4.cso.uiuc.edu (Christopher J Burian) writes:

>>>You'll make everyone happy by hopping down from your high horse and restoring
>>>the newsgroups. 

>> Except, of course, people who respect the rights of the legitimate owners
>> of copyrighted pictures.  In case you haven't noticed, opinion on this
>> subject is far from unanimous, so please don't speak for "everyone".

>> Stay on your "high horse" Paul-- It takes courage to make a stand against
>> theft!

I see two distinct arguments surrounding the removal of the groups.  One is
that copyrights are being violated.  The other is that only some of the
groups are being removed and that the copyright problem is only "discovered"
in the erotica groups.

I think that those who scan the works of others and post them to the net
should be procecuted.  The owner of the copyright should find the violators
and give them hell!  This goes for people who capture sounds of Bart Simpson
or Captain Kirk and post to ab.sounds.misc.  It goes for people who use
their hand scanners to copy articles from the Wall Street Journal and post
them to soc.men!  It's wrong to steal another's work, pure and simple, no
matter where it occurs.  The users of the net (and ESPECIALLY abp.*) need
to address this matter.  It should not be tolerated.

But it's up to the owner of the copyright to do something about it.

Of course, if someone tells Playboy that "Joe User" posted Playboy's
pictures to abpe, that's "Joe User's" tough luck when Playboy's lawyers
decends upon him.  It's also "Joe User's" tough luck if the Student Judical
Board expells him from the University for misuse of the computer center too.
I have no sympathy for those who steal the works of others, whether it is a
GIF picture or a Master's Thesis.

But it's also wrong to remove only one group because of "copyright violations"
while allowing the others to exist.  It tends to make people believe that
there is a hidden agenda.

For those people who want to see the newsgroup restored at their university
site, I'd recommend that they persue the matter in the following ways:

1)      Inquire with your computer facility what the written policy is over
        newsgroup selection/removal.  It's possible that there is no formal 
        policy and this is an arbitrary decision of the person(s) who runs the
        news server.  If there is no formal policy, the decision can be
        challanged elsewhere in the university.  You can get faculty
        involved if this is the arbitrary decision of a staff member.
        Staff members don't have the political clout that tenured faculty do.

2)      Go to your Library Science department (or just the campus library)
        and speak with a faculty member or the dean of the school of Library
        Science.  Explain what USENET is and what newsgroups are.

        Once you've done that, explain that a newsgroup has been removed
        that was being used by the university community.

        Explain that the newsgroup has been removed supposedly due to
        copyright violations.  Now explain that other groups that "suffer"
        from the same "problem" have been left intact.  Explain that you
        believe the group was removed because the group was devoted to
        erotica and that you believe that this was the driving factor in the
        removal of the group, not the supposed copyright violations.
        
        In other words, the group has been removed because it deals with
        erotica -- it's being censored pure and simple.  That ought to
        get a Library Science faculty member's attention.

        Librarians are very sensitive to censorship.  People are "offended"
        all the time and "demand" that books must be removed from their
        library.  They have review committees that deal with these issues.
        
        Some universities view USENET newsgroups in the same light as a book
        in library.  At the very least, you may be successful in getting the
        entire newgroup selection process to be reviewed.  It's possible
        that there is no current policy in place for newsgroup
        selection/removal.

3)      Bring this matter up to your local campus newspaper.  (I'd use this
        as a last resort.  See what the Library Science faculty can do first).


As the news admin for the Univeristy of Pittsburgh, I have been approached by
people who are offended by abpe (or "alt.sex.naked-women" as it is sometimes
mis-identified).  They want the group pulled because of the nudity.  They
tell me it is wrong to have nude pictures available.  Some men have told me
that abpe is "offensive to women".  

I tell them to go to the university library and see that the library carries
not only Playboy but also Penthouse.  They have back issues well into the
1960s of Playboy.  If it's wrong to have nude pictures available, they
better tell the libraries first.

Sometimes people complain about the copyright violations in abpe.  I then
scan my news spool for articles that begin "Posted without permission".
When I find some of these in half a dozen "regular" newsgroups, I ask:
"Should I remove the newsgroups where these articles were posted?"

Goodbye soc.men?

        So long alt.feminism?

                Bon voyage rec.humor?


I don't feel that I have the mandate to censor newsgroups here at the
University of Pittsburgh.  Certainly, my manager has never told me that I
should be removing already established newsgroups.  I add any newsgroup
that is successfully voted in or is "successfully" discussed in alt.config
and contains a charter.  (Groups like "alt.wesley.die.die.die" don't have
a chance, however).  That's the policy that I'm working under.

A faculty committee has been established to address the issue of what
newsgroups to carry and what ones to drop.  The faculty of the University
are the ones who should make such a decision; and I'm happy to leave it to
them.  I'd be very concerned about keeping my job if I arbitrarily removed
groups without the support of the faculty and my management.  I'd be on very
thin ice indeed.  I think any staff member would be.

So for those of you who want to see your newsgroups restored, you have a
couple of options to persue.  If the decision to remove abpe comes with the
blessing of the faculty, your probably out of luck.  But, given that some
groups are still intact, I'd bet that the decision to pull the group does
NOT come from the faculty.  And therein exists your chance to get the
decision reversed.

But it's going to be up to you.  It's going to be an uphill battle.  If you
don't have the wherewithal to persue this, it's not going to happen.
Flaming here on the net will accomplish nothing.  Get your buns over to your
Library Science department.

Terry

