From caf-talk Caf Dec 12 12:13:56 1993
Newsgroups: alt.politics.correct
Subject:  Censoring "Terry Parks"
Date: 11 Dec 1993 23:18:00 -0500
Message-ID: <2ee61oINNjqs@rachmaninov.cs.unc.edu>

[I said]
   >  The issue is what
   >THIS UNIVERSITY is about.
In article <2e8vjs$rsj@panix.com> sethb@panix.com (Seth Breidbart) replied:
 :   Censorship from a university is even worse.  In the free marketplace
 :   of ideas, truth will win out.

You are displaying a tragic lack of understanding of the difference
between the graduate and the undergraduate halves of a university.
At the graduate level, OF COURSE you have to have a marketplace of
ideas.  You are researching things that are not fully understood,
and a fresh perspective may in fact be critical to a breakthrough.
You're pushing the envelope.  Plus, even worse (this is old
news to anyone who's read T.S. Kuhn on scientific revolutions),
that whole community is just incestuously conformist.  Half the
time, when a newer, better, theory comes along, even WITH all
the lip service to a marketplace of ideas, the only way it 
achieves real acceptance is after the people who were trained
in the old method DIE OFF.  They've got too much reputation
invested in the old method.  A vigilant defense of academic and
intellectual freedom is absolutely critical to the maintenance
of intellectual vitality, the prevention of some institutional
analogue of arteriosclerosis, in any graduate department.

BUT

At the undergraduate level, things are ENTIRELY different.
You are teaching what everybody KNOWS already, what is BEYOND
dispute.  You cannot have anybody who intends to teach that
the earth is flat in an undergraduate geology department.
That isn't even a QUESTION of free speech, or of a marketplace
of ideas.  Undergraduates aren't in school for the purpose of
buying what they feel like from the market.  They're in
school because the volume of relevant known facts is so big that 
they have to be.  It is not possible to question everything
all the time.  That gets even truer in this age of information
explosion: filter or drown.  You have to get good at dismissing
garbage efficiently.  The mere fact that half the American demos
is ignorant enough to think an issue is debatable does not make
it so.  Everybody who DESERVED a baccalaureate degree knows better.

 :   Nobody is saying you can't disagree and argue with Terry Parks; don't
 :   we all do so?

No, but you were saying I couldn't forcibly oppose felonious
advocacy, which I would reserve the right to do.  More importantly,
there IS someone who says I can't argue and disagree with
Terry Parks: MALTHUS.  Time/life/resources are FINITE; they 
are in SHORT supply; they cannot be WASTED on activities as
suboptimal as arguing with Terry Parks.  If I argue, I lose my
time; if I don't argue, I lose the election; either way, I have
to pay a loss, and I henceforth refuse to pay.

 :   Now suppose that Glenn Cooley or whoever became a student at UNC and
 :   got an account; would you attempt to prevent him from posting from his
 :   own legitimate account?

This is moot.
It is a state university, so nothing could be done.
Coming where I come from, I would be much more likely to
help him sue the university if THEY tried to do anything.
Any "attempts to prevent" that I might make are not ones
that I would allow the law to find out about, let alone
the net.

The relevant question is what would I do if it were
a private university and I were in charge.
The answer depends on the manner in which he was
willing to discuss the issue privately. The group
dynamics of that could get very psychologically
intense.  In an oral context you are not nearly as
limited by bandwidth.  "Parks"/Cooley made a "musical
chairs" point that you agree with, for example.
Given that the only difference between publicly and
loudly insisting on that point and publicly and loudly
insisting that black colleges are racist is a short
trip to the library, I would say that any sanction
that could be applied to Parks for this behavior
would apply equally well to you.

It is entirely possible that the fool's position
may actually evolve as a result of discovering
that most private black colleges were founded by
white missionaries. S/he may actually have been
embarrassed that s/he could flaunt his/her TOTAL IGNORANCE
to the world.  S/he may realize that things are not
always what they seem.  S/he may question his/her
beliefs for a change, or do some reading to see
if there is anything ELSE that is different from
the way it superficially looks.  I haven't done
any such thing since Iran in 1980, & I doubt you have either.
-- 
Carl Kadie -- I do not represent any organization; this is just me.
